FINDINGS:

1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:2272-2280 — module-member-write repair removes the published function purely by token position, with no reachability/scope check: `if member_writes.contains(&index) { if let Some(functions) = declared_module_functions.get_mut(&module) { functions.remove(name); } }`. A `set M.f to 2` written inside an uncalled function body (or an untaken branch) is still removed from the published table at its source index, so every later `M.f`/`M.f()` in the file resolves to `None` even though the runtime never executed the write and `M.f` still holds the function. This contradicts the object-parent pass just above (src/lsp.rs:2171-2180), which explicitly refuses to treat declarations inside an uncalled function body or untaken branch as live. Concrete fix: record the frame/scope of the write and only apply the removal when the write is reachable in the same runtime frame (or at least not inside a function/branch that cannot have run), mirroring the visibility test used for `extends`. VERIFIED: traced the path — `member_writes` is populated in the `TokenKind::Set` branch at src/lsp.rs:1842-1843 and consumed unconditionally at src/lsp.rs:2276; the only guard is set membership, not scope. The runtime reachability claim needs an interpreter run to confirm.

2. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:1838-1846 — the member-write branch `break`s out of the name loop, so it bypasses the type-annotation handling at src/lsp.rs:1962-1967 (`skip_type_expression(..., &mut type_names)`). For a typed member write such as `set Person.name: Text to …`, `Text` is never placed in `type_names`, so the ordinary identifier pass can resolve a same-spelled variable/parameter as a use of `Text` instead of recognising it as a type annotation. Concrete fix: after recording `member_writes`, skip the `: Type` segment (or fall through to the existing `skip_type_expression` logic) before breaking. Not runtime-verified; the source shape is not covered by any test.

3. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:1838 — the new member-write branch is tested only for a top-level object write and a write inside an `if` branch (tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:4857-4981) and a top-level module/alias write (tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:4366-4433). Missing cases: `constant M.f`, `set this.name` inside a method, a write inside a function/loop/module body (the branch’s frame handling is distinct there, and `runtime_scopes`/`frames` state is live), and a write to a forward-referenced receiver. Explicit missing tests: `edge_constant_module_member_write_replaces_the_published_function`, `edge_member_assignment_through_this_inside_a_method`, `edge_member_assignment_inside_a_function_body`, `edge_member_assignment_before_a_later_object_declaration`. Per the edge-case-gap rule a branch with only one side covered is a finding.

